Modeling changes of the Keymaster TA of linaro-swg/kmgk (keymaster/ta: include/operations.h,
operations.c, auth.c and keystore_ta.c as published, concatenated in this order), applied before
tools/lang-preprocess.py preprocesses it.  The model keeps the state of the operations of the TA
(the operation table of operations.c, which outlives the commands and the sessions) and the paths
of begin, update, finish and abort through it, with their authorization checks, for HMAC keys.
Everything else is left out or simplified; the [ta.keymaster] table of the analysis files only says what to
preprocess.

1. Types.  The types of hardware/keymaster_defs.h and hw_auth_token.h (not in the repository) that
   the model needs are here.  A key blob is a struct with the key data (one buffer) and the
   authorization tags that the operations use (KM_TAG_USER_SECURE_ID, KM_TAG_USER_AUTH_TYPE,
   KM_TAG_NO_AUTH_REQUIRED, KM_TAG_AUTH_TIMEOUT), not the encrypted blob of upstream: so there is
   no TA_restore_key (key blob decryption under the master key and parameter parsing).  A byte
   array is one buffer (the HMAC of an auth token).
2. An operation (keymaster_operation_t) keeps the fields that the model uses: the key blob, the
   handle, the purpose, the time of the last access, the TEE operation, min_sec and do_auth.  The
   TEE operation and the time are values: upstream keeps a pointer to a TEE_Malloc'd handle, and a
   pointer to a local TEE_Time (last_access = &cur_t, which dangles once the function returns), so
   TA_kill_old_operation compares stale stack data; the model compares the times.  The nonce, the
   buffered data, the digest and the AES/RSA/EC fields are left out.
3. The operation table (static keymaster_operation_t operations[20]) is one static array per field,
   as the model has no arrays of structs, with two operations instead of 20: the scenarios have at
   most two at a time.  The functions of operations.c read and write the fields in the arrays; the
   timer of a key with KM_TAG_MIN_SECONDS_BETWEEN_OPS (TA_trigger_timer) is left out (min_sec is
   UNDEFINED in the model).  The table is a global of the TA, so the model keeps it from one
   command to the next as the instance memory of the TA, shared by all the sessions of the
   instance (the TA is single-instance, multi-session), as upstream does.
4. auth.c.  The auth token key is the persistent object AUTH_TOKEN_KEY_ID (a constant of the model,
   auth_token_key_id[] upstream).  The signature of an auth token is the HMAC of its user id, under
   the auth token key: upstream signs the whole token but the HMAC, which the model cannot
   serialize; the Gatekeeper TA of the model signs the same.  TA_check_auth_token compares the
   user id with the one secure id of the key, and the authenticator type for equality (upstream:
   a bitwise and with the type of the key, in network byte order).  As upstream, it does not compare
   the challenge of the token with the operation handle, nor the time of the token with the
   timeout of the key.  TA_do_auth reads the tags of the key blob instead of a parameter set; a
   missing token is NULL.
5. The commands.  TA_begin, TA_update, TA_finish and TA_abort take a request struct and a response
   struct (the fields that upstream deserializes and serializes) instead of the serialized buffers;
   the buffer checks go.  TA_InvokeCommandEntryPoint, the entry point of the model, passes the
   request (params[0]) and the response (params[1]), and keeps these four commands: the others
   (configuration, key generation, import and export, attestation, ...) get its default answer,
   KM_ERROR_UNIMPLEMENTED.  TA_begin keeps the order of upstream: the key object, then the
   checks of TA_check_params for the purpose (sign or verify) and for authorization, then the HMAC
   operation (TA_create_operation), the random handle and TA_start_operation.  The authorization
   checks are those of TA_check_params: a key with NO_AUTH_REQUIRED needs nothing; a key with a
   secure id and AUTH_TIMEOUT needs a valid token at begin and is not checked again (do_auth is
   false); a key with a secure id and no AUTH_TIMEOUT gets do_auth, but begin then fails with
   KM_ERROR_KEY_USER_NOT_AUTHENTICATED (upstream's else branch), so per-operation authorization
   never succeeds.  TA_update and TA_finish keep the order of upstream: the operation, TA_do_auth
   when do_auth, then the HMAC (TEE_MACUpdate, TEE_MACComputeFinal for sign, TEE_MACCompareFinal
   for verify); update aborts the operation on an error, finish always.

The entry points are not modeled: TA_CreateEntryPoint (which resets the table and creates the
secret key and the auth token key when they are missing) and the session entry points.  As
upstream, TA_CloseSessionEntryPoint does nothing: an operation outlives the session that began it.
The auth token key is in the storage of the TA in the initial state of a scenario.

